<main>
<section class="intro section">
    <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">A heritage town</p>
        <h2>More than a temple</h2>
        <p>
            Vontimitta is known especially for the Sri Kodandarama Swamy Temple, a major
            Rama shrine in YSR Kadapa district. The Andhra Pradesh district administration
            describes the temple as a major architectural landmark, with a large sculpted
            mandapam of 32 pillars and images of Rama, Sita and Lakshmana carved from a
            single piece of rock.
        </p>
        <p>
            The temple is about 25 km from Kadapa and stands as a nationally important
            protected monument. Its present architectural form is strongly associated with
            the Chola and Vijayanagara traditions, with the surviving complex showing the
            mature stone craftsmanship of the Vijayanagara period.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="fact-card">
        <div><span>Location</span><strong>Vontimitta, YSR Kadapa</strong></div>
        <div><span>Deity</span><strong>Sri Kodandarama Swamy</strong></div>
        <div><span>Architecture</span><strong>Vijayanagara / Dravidian</strong></div>
        <div><span>Distance</span><strong>~25 km from Kadapa</strong></div>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="history" class="section dark-section">
    <p class="eyebrow">01 — History</p>
    <h2>From an older sacred tradition to a monumental 16th-century complex</h2>

    <div class="timeline">
        <article>
            <span class="year">Before the 16th century</span>
            <h3>Older sacred memory</h3>
            <p>
                Local tradition connects Vontimitta with the Ramayana and with sacred water
                spots such as Ramathirtham and Lakshmanathirtham. These are religious traditions,
                not the same thing as securely dated archaeological evidence.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article>
            <span class="year">16th century</span>
            <h3>Vijayanagara-era expansion</h3>
            <p>
                The present monumental temple is generally dated to the 16th century.
                The district administration attributes the temple to Chola and Vijayanagara
                kings. Architectural and inscriptional evidence points particularly to a
                major Vijayanagara-period development of the complex.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article>
            <span class="year">1554–1558 CE</span>
            <h3>Inscriptional evidence</h3>
            <p>
                Surviving inscriptional evidence associated with the temple records gifts
                connected with the gopura and prakara in this period. These inscriptions
                provide a firmer historical anchor than later oral legends.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article>
            <span class="year">17th century</span>
            <h3>Seen by European travellers</h3>
            <p>
                The French traveller Jean-Baptiste Tavernier visited the temple in 1652.
                Accounts of his visit are often cited for his admiration of the monument's
                scale and architectural beauty.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article>
            <span class="year">Modern period</span>
            <h3>Protected heritage and living worship</h3>
            <p>
                The temple and adjoining structures are recognized as a monument of national
                importance. The shrine continues to function as a place of worship while its
                architectural heritage is preserved.
            </p>
        </article>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="legend" class="section">
    <div class="two-col">
        <div>
            <p class="eyebrow">02 — Sthala Purana & legend</p>
            <h2>Vontudu and Mittudu</h2>
            <p>
                One of Vontimitta's best-known legends tells of two men, Vontudu and Mittudu,
                traditionally described as robbers who became devoted to Lord Rama. According
                to the story, their devotion led them to build the temple and they were
                transformed into stone guardians.
            </p>
            <div class="note">
                <strong>Important:</strong>
                This is a sacred/local legend. It should not be presented as a proven
                archaeological fact. The website deliberately separates devotional tradition
                from dated historical evidence.
            </div>
        </div>
        <img class="rounded-image" src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Vontimitta_Temple.jpg"
             alt="Sri Kodandarama Swamy Temple, Vontimitta">
    </div>
</section>

