        <figure>
            <img src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/16th_century_Kodandarama_temple,_Vontimitta,_Andhra_Pradesh_India_-_24.jpg" alt="Vontimitta temple carved details">
            <figcaption>Carved architectural details</figcaption>
        </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="architecture-grid">
        <div>
            <h3>32-pillar Madhya Ranga Mandapam</h3>
            <p>
                The district administration highlights the beautifully sculpted 32-pillar
                mandapam leading toward the inner chamber and sanctum.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div>
            <h3>Ramayana imagery</h3>
            <p>
                The pillars and walls carry intricate relief work associated with the
                Vijayanagara artistic vocabulary, including scenes and motifs connected
                with Rama and the wider Vaishnava tradition.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div>
            <h3>Single-rock icons</h3>
            <p>
                Rama, Sita and Lakshmana are traditionally described by the district
                administration as being carved from a single piece of rock.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="people" class="section dark-section">
    <p class="eyebrow">04 — Literature & culture</p>
    <h2>Vontimitta in the Telugu literary imagination</h2>

    <div class="people-grid">
        <article>
            <h3>Bammera Pothana</h3>
            <p>
                The Kadapa district administration identifies Vontimitta with Bammera Pothana,
                the celebrated Telugu poet associated with the Andhra Maha Bhagavatamu.
                This connection is an important part of the town's literary identity.
            </p>
        </article>
        <article>
            <h3>Vavilakolanu Subba Rao</h3>
            <p>
                Vavilakolanu Subba Rao, popularly known as Andhra Valmiki, is associated with
                the Telugu Ramayana tradition and is remembered in connection with Vontimitta.
            </p>
        </article>
        <article>
            <h3>Annamacharya tradition</h3>
            <p>
                Traditional accounts also associate the saint-poet Tallapaka Annamacharya
                with visits to the shrine and devotional compositions.
            </p>
        </article>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="festival" class="section festival">
    <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">05 — Living tradition</p>
        <h2>Sri Rama Navami & Brahmotsavam</h2>
        <p>
            Vontimitta is not merely an archaeological monument. Sri Rama Navami and the
            associated Brahmotsavam continue to bring the temple alive with ritual,
            processions and large gatherings of devotees.
        </p>
        <p>
            In the modern era, Vontimitta became especially prominent for state-level
            Sri Rama Navami celebrations after the reorganization of Andhra Pradesh.
            The district administration continues to publish development and festival
            arrangements for the shrine.
        </p>
    </div>
    <div class="festival-card">
        <div class="festival-icon">ॐ</div>
        <h3>Heritage that is still alive</h3>
        <p>Worship • Architecture • Literature • Festival • Community</p>
    </div>
</section>

